schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: "Stewardship Roles for a Knowledge Commons That Outlives You"
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: guide
  summary: >-
    A guide on naming, assigning, and handing off the five stewardship
    roles (Editor, Curator, Facilitator, Maintainer, Onboarder) a knowledge
    commons needs, how to measure fragility via the Contributor Absence
    Factor (Bus Factor), and how to wire roles into existing tooling
    (CODEOWNERS, all-contributors, GOVERNANCE.md) so succession survives
    turnover.
  audience: small teams running a docs/GitHub-based knowledge commons
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      Roles "written in a Notion page nobody opens are decoration"; the
      article argues roles only become real when enforced by tooling
      (CODEOWNERS, bots) rather than documented as intent.
  related_concepts:
    - stewardship-knowledge-commons
    - contributor-absence-factor-bus-factor
  related_resources:
    - codeowners
    - all-contributors
    - gravity-dao
    - protocol-guild
    - wikimedia-stewards
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/stewardship-roles.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  notes: >-
    assessed-but-excluded: the article addresses a reader named "Sol"
    directly ("Sol, this is where it gets concrete") — this reads as an
    audience persona / direct-address device, not a real private
    individual's personal information being exposed, so no person-node or
    public-use-boundary created for it. No ecological/MRV/funding-decision
    content triggering high_risk.
